        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.124,
            0.527,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": "n **Set up an electric circuit as shown in Fig. 3.2.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.139,
            0.53,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "**Place the metal to be tested in the circuit between terminals A and B as shown.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.169,
            0.529,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "**Does the bulb glow? What does this indicate?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.205,
            0.596,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "**You must have seen that the wires that carry current in your homes have a coating of polyvinylchloride (PVC) or a rubber-like material. Why are electric wires coated with such substances?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.603,
            0.088,
            0.92,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.73,
                0.239,
                0.913,
                0.283
              ],
              "content": "Figure 3.2 Metals are good conductors of electricity."
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.269,
            0.724,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "**What happens when metals strike a hard surface? Do they produce a sound? The metals that produce a sound on striking a hard surface are said to be sonorous. Can you now say why school bells are made of metals?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.349,
            0.291,
            0.369
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.1.2 Non-metals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.378,
            0.723,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "**In the previous Class you have learnt that there are very few non-metals as compared to metals. Some of the examples of non-metals are carbon, sulphur, iodine, oxygen, hydrogen, etc. The non-metals are either solids or gases except bromine which is a liquid.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.442,
            0.724,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "**Do non-metals also have physical properties similar to that of metals? Let us find out.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.494,
            0.297,
            0.517
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 3.7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.53,
            0.695,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "n **Collect samples of carbon (coal or graphite), sulphur and iodine.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.545,
            0.698,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "**Carry out the Activities 3.1 to 3.4 and 3.6 with these non-metals and record your observations.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.595,
            0.718,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": "**Compile your observations regarding metals and non-metals in Table 3.1.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.653,
            0.92,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": "| Element | Symbol | Type ofsurface | Hardness | Malleability | Ductility | ConductsElectricity | Sonority |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.102,
                0.634,
                0.195,
                0.65
              ],
              "content": "Table 3.1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_table_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.777,
            0.724,
            0.843
          ],
          "content": "**On the bases of the observations recorded in Table 3.1, discuss the general physical properties of metals and non-metals in the class. You must have concluded that we cannot group elements according to their physical properties alone, as there are many exceptions. For example –**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.848,
            0.723,
            0.882
          ],
          "content": "**(i) All metals except mercury exist as solids at room temperature. In Activity 3.5, you have observed that metals have high melting**"
        },
